\label{unitaries}
Given $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and a permutation $\pi\in S_{n}$, let $U[\pi]$ denote the permutation unitary in $M_{n}$ obtained from the identity matrix by moving the $i$th row to the $\pi(i)$th row. If we are given a transposition $(i \ j) \in S_{n}$, let $u_{(i \  j)}\colon[0,1]\to \mathcal{U}(M_{n})$ denote a continuous path of unitaries with the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item $u_{(i \ j)}(0)=1_{n}$;
	\item $u_{(i \ j)}(1)=U[(i \ j)]$;
	\item for all $0\leq \theta\leq 1$, $u_{(i \ j)}(\theta)$ may only differ from the identity matrix at entries $(i,i)$, $(i,j)$, $(j, i)$, and $(j,j)$. 
\end{enumerate}

